    <!-- Onboarding -->
    <string name="ob1_eyebrow">Keeper</string>
    <string name="ob1_title">You keep the lighthouse</string>
    <string name="ob1_text">At night, ships come out of the fog: thoughts. The keeper doesn\'t board them, chase them or try to sink them. The job is to notice, write it in the log and shine the light. Then the ship sails past.</string>
    <string name="ob2_eyebrow">The fleet</string>
    <string name="ob2_title">Every thought gets a ship</string>
    <string name="ob2_text">A thought that keeps coming back gets a ship and a name. “The Sack is on the horizon again” is not the same as “I\'ll be fired”. You weigh each one: boat, schooner, frigate or galleon.</string>
    <string name="ob3_eyebrow">The watch</string>
    <string name="ob3_title">Ships are looked over in the evening</string>
    <string name="ob3_text">During the day, a ship is lit and let pass, or left at anchor. Once a day, at the watch, you look the anchored ones over: weigh them again, write down what cargo they claim to bring, chart what happens if it comes. Not before.</string>
    <string name="ob3_time">Watch at %s</string>
    <string name="ob4_eyebrow">No consolation</string>
    <string name="ob4_title">This lighthouse doesn\'t say “it\'ll be fine”</string>
    <string name="ob4_text">It keeps a log instead: how many cargoes actually arrived, how much lighter a ship was by evening, how long since it last came. Ships whose forecasts keep coming to nothing turn into ghosts. Ships that stop coming end up in bottles on the shelf.</string>
    <string name="ob4_note">Keeper is a tool built on worry postponement, defusion and checking predictions. It doesn\'t replace therapy. If thoughts are getting in the way of living, a CBT therapist can help.</string>
    <string name="ob_done">Light the lamp</string>

    <!-- Hints -->
    <string name="hint_eyebrow">From the keeper\'s notes</string>
    <string name="hint_ok">Understood</string>
    <string name="hint_bay_title">The bay</string>
    <string name="hint_bay">The fog is today\'s load: the more ships and the heavier, the thicker it gets. Lights out on the water are ships at anchor waiting for the watch. The button at the bottom is for when a new one shows.</string>
    <string name="hint_sight_title">Log it in a line</string>
    <string name="hint_sight">Write the thought in your own words, pick or name its ship and weigh it. Don\'t argue with it here. Then either light it and let it pass, or anchor it until the evening.</string>
    <string name="hint_pass_title">Watch from the shore</string>
    <string name="hint_pass">The beam keeps time for your breath: in as it turns toward you, out as it sweeps away. The ship crosses the bay with its thought beneath it. Nothing to do: just watch it go.</string>
    <string name="hint_watch_title">The only place to look a ship over</string>
    <string name="hint_watch">Weigh each anchored ship again: often it\'s smaller by evening. Write down its cargo as a forecast you can check, or chart where it leads. Check the cargo that\'s due. Then close the log.</string>
    <string name="hint_log_title">Your own evidence</string>
    <string name="hint_log">Not statistics about other people: your forecasts, and what came of them. The crates are every cargo checked: full for one that arrived, empty for one that didn\'t.</string>
    <string name="hint_fleet_title">Ships, not facts</string>
    <string name="hint_fleet">Every recurring thought you\'ve named. A ship whose forecasts keep coming to nothing fades into a ghost. One that stops coming for a month goes into a bottle.</string>
    <string name="hint_shelf_title">Ships in bottles</string>
    <string name="hint_shelf">A ship quiet for thirty days is over the horizon. It isn\'t forbidden from coming back: if it does, it simply sails off the shelf again.</string>
    <string name="hint_ship_title">Its page in the log</string>
    <string name="hint_ship">When it comes, where it sails in from, whether it\'s getting smaller, and what its cargo has really been.</string>
    <string name="hint_chart_title">Sound it to the bottom</string>
    <string name="hint_chart">“And if it does, then what?”, again and again, until there\'s nowhere further down. At the bottom, the lifeboat: what you would actually do. Usually the bottom turns out to have a floor.</string>

